    <sys:String x:Key="S_Luminance">Lum.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Lut">Lookup table</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Clarity">Clarity</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ClarityAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ClarityReach">Radius</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ClarityHint">Local contrast: it boosts what a pixel is against its surroundings and leaves flat areas alone. A large radius gives modelling, a small one gives sharpness. Negative takes contrast away.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PassMissingDepth">This file carries no depth pass - the tool rests. In Blender: View Layer, Passes, Data, Z.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PassMissingMotion">This file carries no vector pass - the tool rests. In Blender: View Layer, Passes, Data, Vector (with motion blur off in the render settings).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PassMissingNormal">This file carries no normal pass - the tool rests. In Blender: View Layer, Passes, Data, Normal.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Displace">Displacement</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceWave">Wave</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceLength">Wavelength</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceAngle">Direction</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceSpread">Channel offset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceFromNormal">Normal pass</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceFromMotion">Vector pass</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceFromScreen">Wave only</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ShapeSine">Sine</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ShapeTriangle">Triangle</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ShapeSquare">Square</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ShapeSaw">Sawtooth</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ShapeSlices">Slices (glitch)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ShapeBlocks">Blocks (glitch)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceShapeHint">The first four oscillate - water, heat haze, a picture losing its hold. Slices and blocks jump: bands or rectangles are offset by random amounts, like a broken signal or a damaged file.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceBand">Band height</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceDensity">Density</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceDensityHint">How many of the bands move at all. A real signal fault hits a few lines and leaves the rest - at full density it becomes a pattern.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceSpeed">Speed</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceStill">still</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceSpeedHint">How the displacement moves over the sequence. For waves: waves per frame - 0.05 moves it one wavelength in twenty frames. For slices and blocks: how often the tear changes - 1 every frame, 0.25 every fourth.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceWaveHint">How much follows the wave. 1 entirely, 0 not at all - then the whole picture is simply shifted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplacePhase">Phase</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceWrap">Wrap around</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceWrapHint">What is pushed out at one edge comes back in at the other - like a television that cannot hold the picture. Off: the edge is held and smears into stripes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Reseed">Reroll</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ReseedHint">A new tear with the same settings - different bands, different offsets.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceFromHint">Where the direction comes from. The normal pass lays the distortion along the geometry: a sphere pushes outward, a wall not at all. The vector pass lays it along the motion: what stands still stays put, what moves tears along its path.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DisplaceHint">Needs the chosen pass in the file - without it the tool rests. The wave turns the smooth displacement into bands that push forward and back in turn; the channel offset gives them the colour fringe that reads as interference rather than blur.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Motion">Motion blur</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MotionShutter">Shutter</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MotionSamples">Samples</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MotionHint">Needs the Vector pass in the file. Shutter is the share of the frame time it was open — a half is 180 degrees, as in film. This too is an approximation: a moving object smears within itself but not beyond its edge, because what it would have covered in passing is not in the file.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DepthField">Depth of field</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DepthAperture">Aperture</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DepthFocus">Focus</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DepthHint">Needs a depth pass in the file — Depth, Z, or Mist as a stand-in. Focus is in the scene's own units. It is an approximation: what lies behind a sharp edge is not in the file, so a blurred background cannot creep over a sharp foreground.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Distortion">Distortion</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DistortionAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DistortionScale">Scale</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DistortionHint">Barrel and pincushion: a wide angle bows straight lines outwards, a long lens pulls them in. Straightening a barrel brings the edge into frame — a scale below one enlarges far enough for the empty corners to fall outside.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Chromatic">Colour fringing</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ChromaticAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ChromaticHint">Chromatic aberration: a lens bends blue more than red, so towards the edges the channels no longer meet. Adding it is a look, removing it is a fix — the sign is the direction. Green stays put.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Vignette">Vignette</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_VignetteAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_VignetteMidpoint">Midpoint</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_VignetteRoundness">Roundness</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_VignetteFeather">Feather</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_VignetteHint">A lens falling off towards the edges. It multiplies light and acts before the view transform — which is why it needs no highlight protection: a lamp stays white instead of turning into a grey disc. Roundness runs from diamond through circle to rectangle.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Sort">Pixel sorting</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortFrom">From</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortTo">To</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortLongest">Longest run</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortLongestOff">no limit</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortVertical">Columns</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortVerticalHint">Vertical runs instead of horizontal. Looks like rain.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortDescending">Descending</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortDescendingHint">Large values first. Flips the direction of every gradient.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortByBrightness">by brightness</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortByHue">by hue</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortBySaturation">by saturation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortByThreshold">Runs: threshold</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortByEdges">Runs: edges</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortByRandom">Runs: random lengths</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortIntervalHint">What limits a run. Threshold: everything that lies connected inside the window. Edges: also where brightness jumps - outlines hold, surfaces melt. Random lengths: runs of varying length around a mean.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortByIntensity">by intensity</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortByMinimum">by minimum</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortAngle">Angle</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortAngleHint">The direction of the runs. 0 left to right, 90 top to bottom, and anything in between.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortMean">Mean length</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortLongestAuto">60 px</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortEdge">Edge threshold</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortEdgeHint">How far brightness must jump between neighbours for a run to end there. Low: many edges, short runs. High: only hard outlines hold.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortSkip">Skip</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortSkipHint">How many runs stay unsorted. If every line melts alike the picture looks combed - if some stay, it looks disturbed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortSpeedHint">How often the random parts change over the sequence - random lengths and skipping. 1 every frame, 0.25 every fourth, 0 never.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortCross">Crosswise</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortCrossHint">After the first pass a second one turned by 90 degrees - like Kim Asendorf, columns first, then rows. The picture melts both ways.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortKeyHint">What is compared - and what the threshold means. Brightness turns runs into gradients from dark to light, hue makes bands, saturation separates pale from vivid and leaves the form standing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SortHint">Only pixels BETWEEN the two thresholds are reordered - the window is the strength control. Closed, nothing happens; opened, the picture falls into runs with everything else left standing. Like dithering, it joins on release.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Grain">Film grain</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GrainAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GrainSize">Size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GrainRoughness">Roughness</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GrainColour">Colour</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GrainHint">The one tool that has to change per frame — otherwise it is not grain but dirt on the lens. The throw comes from the frame number in the file name, not from the position in the run: frame 47 always gets the same grain.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Dehaze">Dehaze</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DehazeAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DehazeRadius">Radius</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DehazeHint">Takes away the veil the air adds between subject and camera. It is estimated from the darkest colour channel: where even the darkest is still bright, there is haze over it. White stays white. Adding fog needs distance and belongs to depth haze.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Texture">Texture</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_TextureAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_TextureRadius">Radius</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_TextureHint">Surface detail, in both directions. Unlike clarity it does not let go at the ends — bright skin and dark cloth live exactly there. Unlike sharpening it can run backwards without taking the edges with it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Bloom">Glow</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BloomAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BloomThreshold">Threshold</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BloomRadius">Radius</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BloomHint">Light spreading beyond its own edge. The threshold is in scene-linear light: 1 is a white surface, and above it sit lamps and speculars. That is why glow acts before the view transform — behind it, a lamp and the sun are both just white.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Halation">Halation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_HalationAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_HalationThreshold">Threshold</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_HalationRadius">Radius</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_HalationTint">Tint</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_HalationHint">The warm fringe around highlights: in film the light passes through the emulsion, reflects off the base and exposes it a second time. Tighter than glow and always warm — the tint runs from deep red into orange.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_LocalOnFinal">These tools act on the finished picture, not on a single layer. The local ones need a pixel's neighbourhood, and that is only settled once the stack has been composed. Vignette and grain belong to the camera, not to a layer inside it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Noise">Noise reduction</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_NoiseLuma">Luminance</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_NoiseColour">Colour</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_NoiseThreshold">Threshold</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_NoiseHint">Smooths where a pixel differs little from its surroundings and leaves edges alone. Colour takes far more than luminance does, because fine colour detail is rare. Against coarse noise from too few samples, only rendering helps.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Sharpen">Sharpen</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SharpenAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SharpenRadius">Radius</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SharpenThreshold">Threshold</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SharpenHint">The same as clarity at a small radius — and on luminance only, so coloured edges do not get a coloured fringe. The threshold leaves fine noise alone instead of raising it first.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_NoLut">No table selected</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Strength">Strength</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PickLut">Choose a table</sys:String>

    <!-- ================================================================ Layers -->
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Layers">Layers</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_LayersHint">Blender does not split the image into summands but into light times colour. “All passes” builds the stack so it rebuilds exactly the rendered image – any other mode is an intervention.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_AddLayer">Add a pass as a layer</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_AddAllPasses">All passes – rebuild the image</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_AddAdjustment">Adjustment layer</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_AddImage">Image …</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_AddGroup">Group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Place">Placement</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PlaceX">Horizontal</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PlaceY">Vertical</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PlaceScale">Size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PlaceRotation">Rotation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Crop">Crop</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CropLeft">Left</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CropTop">Top</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CropRight">Right</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CropBottom">Bottom</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PlaceReset">reset</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_OnTop">On top – watermark</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_OnTopHint">Sits above the view transform and the grade: identical in every frame, exactly as it is in the file. An ordinary image layer would go through AgX and be graded along with the picture.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PlaceHint">In fractions of the image, not in pixels – a recipe made at 1080p holds at 4K. Size 1 means fitted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DropHere">Drop an image here</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GroupLayer">Group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MoveIntoGroup">Into the group below</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MoveOutOfGroup">Out of the group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_FollowSequence">Follow the frame number</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PickImage">Choose an image or sequence</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ImageMissing">file missing</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ImageHint">If the file carries a frame number it follows the sequence – frame 47 here means frame 47 there. Two versions on Difference show at a glance what changed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GroupHint">A group passes through: its layers see what lies below it. Mask and opacity apply to all of them together.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_SizeMismatch">different size – skipped</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_AdjustmentLayer">Adjustment</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolsTargetImageHint">The sliders act on the finished picture - after every layer. Masks cannot scope that.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolsTargetNoLayer">Layer</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolsTargetLayerHint">The sliders act on the selected layer only. A mask on it then scopes WHERE the correction applies.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolsTargetImage">Whole picture</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolsTargetLayer">Layer: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_AdjustmentHint">The tools below now belong to this layer. With a clipping mask (↳) it acts on the one layer beneath it only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ClipLayer">Affect only the layer below (clipping mask)</sys:String>

    <!-- ================================================================ Masks -->
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Mask">Mask</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskNone">None</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskLuminance">Brightness of the layer</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskUnderlying">Brightness below</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskPass">Pass</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskGradient">Gradient</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskColour">Colour range</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskScopeColour">Colour</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskScopeColourHint">The mask says WHERE this layer's correction applies. The layer itself stays visible everywhere - as if a copy of it sat on top showing only that area.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskScopeShow">Visibility</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskScopeShowHint">The mask says WHERE the layer shows. Outside it contributes nothing - right for a glow, a watermark, a second shot.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskHue">Hue</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskSpread">Range</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskHintColour">Acts only where this hue sits. Pale colours count for less - to take them in full, pull the white point down.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskShadows">Shadows</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskMidtones">Midtones</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskHighlights">Highlights</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskCryptomatte">Cryptomatte</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CryptoPick">Click the image to choose</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CryptoPickOn">Stop choosing</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CryptoEmpty">Nothing chosen</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CryptoUnknown">unnamed</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CryptoHint">The selection holds for the whole sequence: Blender writes the cryptomatte afresh into every frame, and it knows the object – even once it has moved across the picture.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_CryptoNone">This file carries no cryptomattes. Enable them in Blender under View Layer → Passes → Cryptomatte.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskFrom">From</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskBlack">Black point</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskWhite">White point</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskTo">To</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskSoft">Softness</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskInvert">Invert the mask</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskAngle">Direction</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskCentre">Centre</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskWidth">Width</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskHintLuma">From and To limit where the layer acts. Middle grey sits at 0.50 – so “highlights” start around 0.65.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskHintPass">The pass is the mask – if it lies between 0 and 1 its value goes in unchanged. A pass in its own units, a depth in metres say, is mapped onto its own range: black and white point then mean near and far.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskHintGradient">0° runs left to right, 90° top to bottom. Width 0 is a hard edge.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DuplicateLayer">Duplicate</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_RemoveLayer">Remove</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MoveLayerUp">Move up</sys:String>
