    <sys:String x:Key="S_PickAt">At</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PickByte">Display</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PickLight">Light</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_PickDepth">Distance</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_UseAsFocus">Focus on this</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_UseAsFocusHint">Sets the depth of field's focus to the distance just read. Without a picker all you have is a slider in metres and guesswork - and you are guessing at a number you cannot read anywhere.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_FocusSet">Focused at</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_NoDepthTool">Depth of field has to be switched on for this - it lives under Optics.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Reveal">Show what is hidden</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_RevealHint">Shows what sits in the file's transparent areas - usually the unmasked original, render noise, or remnants of the layer below. At 0 the layer is cleanly cut out, at 100% it covers everywhere. This layer's MASK decides where it shows: a cryptomatte picks an object, a depth pass picks a distance. A cleanly premultiplied file carries black there - then nothing happens.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MatteGroup">Matte</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MatteGroupHint">Two sliders on the same number, pulling opposite ways: the upper one cuts away what is barely there - against the grey veil that hides garbage. The lower one pulls coverage towards full and shows that same garbage on purpose.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_Dither">Dither</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherAmount">Amount</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherLevels">Levels per channel</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherSize">Cell size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherNoise">random</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherNoiseHint">Random instead of ordered: grainy rather than a crosshatch. The threshold is still tied to the position - the same spot gets the same one every pass, otherwise the picture would shimmer while you drag a slider.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherHint">Brings the picture down to a few levels per channel without breaking it into flat areas: the threshold depends on where the pixel is, the step wanders across the surface, and the eye averages it back together. There is no Floyd-Steinberg error diffusion here - that passes the rounding error to its neighbours and would have to walk the pixels in order. Anything above white is left alone.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherOrdered">ordered (Bayer)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherDiffusion">error diffusion</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherPatternHint">Ordered gives a crosshatch that repeats every eight pixels - the look of old screens. Random is grainy without a pattern. The line screen carries brightness in the THICKNESS of the line, like an engraving. Everything below is error diffusion: the rounding error is handed to the neighbours and scatters organically - the look of old printers. Floyd-Steinberg is the yardstick; Atkinson discards a quarter of the error and gives hard, streaked surfaces - the look of the first Macintosh; Jarvis and Stucki spread further and look almost grainy. Error diffusion runs on one thread at full resolution: it stays out while you drag a slider, joins on release, and does not run in the 16-bit export.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherLines">line screen</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherAngle">line angle</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DiffusionKernelHint">Which scheme passes the rounding error on. Floyd-Steinberg is the yardstick: tightly spread and fine. Atkinson is the look of the first Macintosh - it passes on only six eighths of the error and discards the last quarter, so bright areas run all the way to white and dark ones to black, and surfaces come out streaked rather than evenly speckled. Jarvis and Stucki spread over two rows and look almost grainy; Burkes and Sierra sit in between.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherPixels">Cell width</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherPixelsHint">The edge length of one raster dot in pixels. At 1 every pixel is decided on its own - the finest rastering, and at high resolution exactly the one that looks like even noise. Four to eight is where the pattern becomes a pattern again. Above 1 even a place-based pattern runs over the whole frame and therefore only appears on release.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherPixelsTall">Cell height</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherPixelsTallHint">The height of one raster dot, separate from its width. Equal width and height gives dots. Width 8 and height 1 gives horizontal dashes, and neighbouring dashes in a row merge into a line that follows the form - that is how the hatching of old prints comes about. The other way round, narrow and tall, gives vertical dashes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherDuotone">two colours</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherDuotoneHint">Rasters the BRIGHTNESS instead of each channel on its own and maps the result onto two colours - black and the chosen one. Without this, red, green and blue each decide for themselves; on a colour picture they drift apart, and what was a readable silhouette falls into three independent clouds of dots. It is also the only way to get colour here at all: the raster pass runs right at the end, after every colour tool.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherHue">Hue</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_DitherSaturation">Saturation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GroupBasics">Basics</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GroupLight">Light and detail</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GroupOptics">Optics</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GroupFilm">Film</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_GroupTable">Table</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolBrush">Brush</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolBrushHint">Brush (B) - paints a mask by hand. Holding Alt takes it away again. The layer needs a painted mask for this; while painting the mask lies over the picture as a coloured wash, because otherwise you would only see its effect.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_ToolBrushShort">Paints a mask – the first stroke creates a mask layer for it. Right mouse button or Alt erases.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskPainted">Painted</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskLock">all frames</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskLockHint">Locked, one painting applies to the whole sequence. Unlocked, each frame gets its own - then a single frame can be retouched without touching the others. On unlocking, the painting so far moves to the current frame rather than being discarded.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskLockedNote">One painting for every frame.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskLooseNote">A separate painting per frame - for single-frame retouching.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_MaskLayerName">Mask</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BrushSize">Size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BrushHardness">Hardness</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BrushFlow">Flow</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BrushOpacity">Opacity</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="S_BrushHint">Flow and opacity are not the same: one says how much a SINGLE stroke lays down, the other where even ten strokes stop. At opacity 0.3 the mask stays just under a third however often you go over it. Hold Alt to erase.</sys:String>
</ResourceDictionary>
